## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming complexity with fixed memory, practical heavy-hitter detection, and batch-based processing.

## Key Concepts
- Misra-Gries heavy-hitter algorithm
- Amortized vs per-item cost
- Threshold guarantees with finite counters
- Secondary verification for exact counts

## Code Example
```javascript
class MG {
  constructor(H){ this.H=H; this.counters=new Map(); }
  update(x){
    if (this.counters.has(x)) this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x)+1);
    else if (this.counters.size < this.H) this.counters.set(x,1);
    else {
      for (let k of Array.from(this.counters.keys())) {
        this.counters.set(k, this.counters.get(k)-1);
        if (this.counters.get(k)===0) this.counters.delete(k);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming data structures, heap-based algorithms, and amortized complexity analysis in a real-time processing context.

## Key Concepts
- **Two-heap streaming median:** A max-heap stores the lower half; a min-heap stores the upper half.
- **Rebalancing invariant:** Heap sizes differ by at most one, ensuring the median is accessible in O(1).
- **Time complexity:** O(log n) per element for insertion plus O(log n) for rebalancing.
- **Space complexity:** O(n), proportional to total elements seen.

## Code Example
```javascript
insert(x) {
  if (L.isEmpty() || x <= L.max()) {
    L.insert(x);
  } else {
    R.insert(x);
  }
  rebalance();
}

rebalance() {
  if (L.size() > R.size() + 1) {
    R.insert(L.extractMax());
  } else if (R.size() > L.size() + 1) {
    L.insert(R.extractMin());
  }
}

getMedian() {
  if (L.size() > R.size()) return L.max();
  if (R.size() > L.size()) return R.min();
  return (L.max() + R.min()) / 2;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design streaming algorithms with bounded memory while computing exact results. It captures a common real-world problem: maintaining aggregate statistics over a sliding or growing dataset without storing all historical values.

## Key Concepts

- **Streaming data structures:** Using heaps to maintain top-K elements efficiently
- **Running sum:** Incremental updates avoid recomputing the sum after each batch
- **Time complexity:** O(log K) per element; batch processing takes O(B log K)
- **Memory usage:** O(K) vs O(N) for the exact method that stores all past values

## Code Example

```javascript
function topKSumStream(valueStream, K) {
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  let sum = 0;

  for (const batch of valueStream) {
    for (const v of batch) {
      if (heap.size() < K) {
        heap.push(v);
        sum += v;
      } else if (heap.size() > 0 && v > heap.peek()) {
        sum += v - heap.pop();
        heap.push(v);
      }
    }
    console.log("Top-K sum:", sum);
  }
}
```

## Asymptotic Analysis

| Metric | Our Solution | Exact Method |
|--------|--------------|--------------|
| Per-element update | O(log K) | O(1) |
| Batch time (size B) | O(B log K) | O(B) |
| Memory | O(K) | O(N) |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about fixed-memory distinct counting in streaming data, a common real-world need for analytics in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for cardinality estimation
- Mergeable sketches across batches
- Relative error ~ 1.04/√m; memory fixed by m

## Code Example
```javascript
function hllAdd(registers, x, hash) {
  const m = registers.length;
  const i = hash & (m - 1);
  const w = hash >>> Math.log2(m);
  const rank = leadingZeros(w) + 1;
  registers[i] = Math.max(registers[i], rank);
}
```

